    <div class="page">
        <p id="some_info">
            This page is dedicated to providing relevant and important information giving context on 
            some of the relevant information about this website. It is not all-inclusive, so we 
            encourage you to research on your own terms.
        </p>
        <div id="tag_info" class="info_block">
            <div id="legacybiz">
                <p class="subheading"> Legacy Businesses </p>
                <p> Legacy businesses are places that have been operating for more than 30 years and contribute
                    historical and cultural significance to the identity of San Francisco, as designated by the SF 
                    Government. These businesses, as well as the countless other businesses not formally 
                    recognized by the registry (available 
                    <a href="https://sfgov.maps.arcgis.com/apps/dashboards/471cc5798f8c486fb0f8e9ac8badaf03#"> here</a>)
                    contribute significantly to the identity that builds our local communities. Located all over 
                    the city, these businesses' purposes vary widely, from art galleries to restaurants, but all 
                    are connected by their meaning to the city as a whole. 
                </p>
            </div>

            <div id="HUNs" class="info_block">
                <p class="subheading"> Historically Underserved Neighborhoods (HUNs) </p>
                <p> Historically Underserved Neighborhoods (HUNs) are neighborhoods and areas in San 
                    Francisco that have a larger percentage of lower-income people, and often lack the 
                    same resources and opportunities that other neighborhoods get. Examples of these areas 
                    include the Bayview, with high African American, Hispanic, and Asian populations, as well 
                    as the Tenderloin and Western Addition. Many, if not all of these underserved regions in 
                    SF, have undergone the historical process of redlining in the 1940s by the Home Owners' 
                    Loan Corporation (HOLC), which created significant differences in the housing situation of 
                    citizens as well as their access to loans, which helped create the differences we see in 
                    different neighborhoods today. If you'd like to read more about redlining, please go 
                    <a href= "information#redlining"> 
                    to this relevant subheading. </a>
                </p>
            </div>

            <div id="women_owned" class="info_block">
                <p class="subheading"> Women-owned Businesses </p>
                <p>Women-owned-and-operated businesses are a major part of businesses today, but can often 
                    become overlooked in the face of large-scale industries that directly impact local 
                    businesses. These businesses' success are a testament not only to their perserverance, 
                    but also a historical landmark of the fight gender equality and rejection of the 
                    predominance of male-focused societies.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div id="free" class="info_block">
                <p class="subheading"> Free Places </p>
                <p>Free-to-access businesses are incredibly important to people across all walks of life, 
                    especially those that may struggle with income. It's important to remember that all 
                    people deserve rights to places like parks, gardens, and public art installations -- which 
                    are highlighted in the relevant tags -- and that often, it can bring back a sense of 
                    dignity that may be lost when fighting to survive in increasingly expensive areas.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div id="pub_trans" class="info_block">
                <p class="subheading"> Public Transit </p>
                <p> Public Transit systems are incredibly important to San Francisco as well as cities as 
                    a whole. SF's main methods of transport are the MUNI and the extensive bus system, 
                    and they run throughout the whole city, providing a stable way for many to get across 
                    and around the city.
                 </p>
            </div>
        </div>
        

    <div id="other_info" class="info_block">
        <div id="redlining">
           <p class="subheading"> Redlining </p> 
           <p> Redlining was a process that was outlined by Roosevelt's New Deal, where cities were zoned 
            into areas of economic interest and accessibility to loans. These maps, created by the Home 
            Owner's Loan Corporation (HOLC), clearly followed ethnic enclave and high-percentage minority 
            neighborhoods, which were often the most economically poor. These maps helped define how 
            bankers and other forms of government funded the neighborhoods and influenced their overall 
            prosperity (change word) today. If you'd like more information, feel free to go to 
            <a href= "https://www.sfgate.com/bayarea/article/San-Francisco-segregated-neighborhoods-redlining-10806723.php"> 
            this website </a> or <a href= "https://storymaps.arcgis.com/stories/34f5b07b28fb4dfeaf4e7ab39e6226af"> 
            this site</a>.
        </p>
        </div>
    </div>



